/* =====================================================================
   LAYER 6 — THE SHAMBLES
   The "in-between" band: hell's edge, the carnival hell-town the player
   spawns into (via the Hellmouth) and shops/gears up in. Register: CARNIVAL —
   garish, festive, sinister FUN; the seductive almost-normalcy that is its own
   trap (you could almost forget where you are, which is exactly what the place
   is for). The toxic blurs into the supernatural here. The Cronenberg body-
   horror still tints the attractions (the freak-show, the butcher, the ribcage
   entertainer) — but the dominant note is the fairground, not the abattoir.

   Hub = TheMidway. Enter from L5 (TheThroat stairs → TheMidway). Descend to L7 (the wet
   stair behind HellMarket's stalls → TheGullet). Market ↔ tavern linked by back
   alleys (per the bible). Depths in setup.depthOf (= 6).
   ===================================================================== */


/* ---------- HUB ---------- */

:: TheMidway [carnival]
<<nobr>>
Past the last tooth of the mouth the Shambles open into a midway — a long crooked
fairground of a street under a sky of low living smoke lit carnival-bright from
below, strung with bunting that is not quite bunting and lined with stalls and
shows and games and the constant warm roar of a crowd having, against everything,
a wonderful time. Barkers call. Bells ring. Somewhere a calliope plays a tune you
half-know, slightly wrong. It is, and this is the whole horror of it, *fun* — a
real, pulling, festive fun — and the having of it costs exactly as much as you let
it, which down here is everything.
<</nobr>>

<<if $corruption gte 70>>
You have stopped feeling watched and started feeling *welcome*, and you move down
the midway with the easy gait of a regular, nodding to barkers, and the worst of it
is how good it feels to be somewhere that's glad you came.
<</if>>

<<linkreplace "Stop a while. Let the fun just... have you.">>
<<corrupt "torso" "pig" 5>>
You stop walking and let the midway close over you like warm water — the noise, the lights, the
laughing crowd so glad you came — and for a long lovely lost stretch you are not trying to leave
at all, just //here//, just having a wonderful time, and when you surface a little of the place has
settled into you and did not want to surface with you.
<</linkreplace>>

<<link "A market of stalls, where they sell the way out">><<goto "HellMarket">><</link>>
<<link "A loud warm tavern, off the midway">><<goto "HellTavern">><</link>>
<<link "The big top, at the midway's heart">><<goto "TheBigTop">><</link>>
<<link "A row of games and barkers">><<goto "TheGames">><</link>>
<<link "A butcher's stall, doing brisk trade">><<goto "TheButchersStall">><</link>>
<<link "A quiet shuttered booth, off to one side">><<goto "TheQuietBooth">><</link>>
<<link "Back up the stairs, to the back of the throat">><<goto "TheThroat">><</link>>


/* ---------- THE MARKET + TAVERN (moved from core; the gear + the descent) ---------- */

:: HellMarket [carnival]
<<nobr>>
The market is a riot of stalls under a low ceiling of living smoke. Things with
too many joints haggle over things you don't have names for; a butcher's stall
sells cuts you recognize and wish you didn't; somewhere a choir of children that
are not children is singing a jingle for somebody's brand of lamp oil. Down here
they trade in coins, mostly — little cold discs stamped with a face that follows
you. But the best stock has a different price, and the merchants are always,
always happy to take it.
<</nobr>>

A merchant the shape of a folded coat leans across its counter. //First time?//
it says. //You've the look of someone who wants to leave. Everyone who wants to
leave needs rope. Funny how that works.//

<<if setup.has("rope")>>
You already have the coil of rope looped over your shoulder.
<<else>>
<<if $coins gte 5>>
<<link "Buy the coil of rope (5 coins)">><<set $coins to $coins - 5>><<acquire "rope">><<goto "HellMarket">><</link>>
<</if>>
<<set _tlbl to "Buy the rope with a piece of yourself instead (" + setup.tollTag("torso", "pig", 20) + ")">><<link _tlbl>><<corrupt "torso" "pig" 20>><<acquire "rope">><<goto "HellMarket">><</link>>
<<if $coins lt 5>>//Short on coin?// the merchant smiles. //I'll always take the other thing. It's a foolish trade. I do love a foolish trade.//<</if>>
<</if>>

<<if not setup.has("hook")>>
<<if $coins gte 8>>
<<link "Buy a rusted gaff — a weapon (8 coins)">><<set $coins to $coins - 8>><<acquire "hook">><<goto "HellMarket">><</link>>
<<else>>(A rusted gaff hangs on a hook: 8 coins.)<</if>>
<</if>>

<<if not setup.has("leathers")>>
<<if $coins gte 8>>
<<link "Buy tanned leathers — armor (8 coins)">><<set $coins to $coins - 8>><<acquire "leathers">><<goto "HellMarket">><</link>>
<<else>>(A suit of tanned leathers, of indeterminate origin: 8 coins.)<</if>>
<</if>>

<<if not setup.has("knife")>>
<<set _tlbl to "Take the flensing knife — it costs only something of you (" + setup.tollTag("torso", "pig", 30) + ")">><<link _tlbl>><<corrupt "torso" "pig" 30>><<acquire "knife">><<goto "HellMarket">><</link>>
<</if>>

/* grave-moss salve — a stackable combat consumable (Items button). Direct push, not
   <<acquire>>, so you can carry more than one. */
<<if $coins gte 4>>
<<link "Buy a wad of grave-moss salve — heals you mid-fight (4 coins)">><<set $coins to $coins - 4>><<run $inventory.push("salve")>><<goto "HellMarket">><</link>>
<<else>>(A wad of pale grave-moss salve, for your hurts in a fight: 4 coins.)<</if>>

/* per-hazard pass-clean tools — for the dirty shortcuts down below (waders/gas-mask). */
<<if not setup.has("waders")>>
<<if $coins gte 10>><<link "Buy rubber waders — for wading the foul ways (10 coins)">><<set $coins to $coins - 10>><<acquire "waders">><<goto "HellMarket">><</link>>
<<else>>(Chest-high rubber waders, perished but whole: 10 coins.)<</if>>
<</if>>
<<if not setup.has("gasmask")>>
<<if $coins gte 12>><<link "Buy an oilskin hood — for the bad-air ways (12 coins)">><<set $coins to $coins - 12>><<acquire "gasmask">><<goto "HellMarket">><</link>>
<<else>>(A hooded oilskin with glass eyes, for foul air: 12 coins.)<</if>>
<</if>>

A little further on, two larger stalls do steadier trade: a smith's, hung with
edged and blunt iron, and a trinket-monger's table glittering with rings and charms.

<<link "Browse the smith's stall — weapons and armour">><<goto "TheSmithStall">><</link>>
<<link "Pick over the trinket-monger's table — rings, charms, boots">><<goto "TheTrinketStall">><</link>>

<<link "Slip down a back alley to the tavern">><<goto "TheBackAlleys">><</link>>
<<link "Down a wet stair behind the stalls, deeper — into the belly">><<goto "TheGullet">><</link>>
<<link "Out to the midway">><<goto "TheMidway">><</link>>


:: TheSmithStall [carnival]
/* Data-driven gear shop (DIRECTION-EQUIPMENT). Renders setup.MARKET_WEAPONS / MARKET_ARMOR.
   Items show via itemDisplayName (cursed bargains read as a suspiciously cheap high tier — the
   tell), brief via itemBrief ("?" until identified). setup.buy handles coin + pack; plain gear
   auto-equips into an empty slot, anything with hidden props waits for the Equipment screen. */
<<nobr>>
The smith's stall is a furnace of orange light and ringing iron, hung wall to wall and
post to post with edged and blunt things on hooks, so many of them that the heat off the
forge sets the whole armoury swaying and clinking like a wind-chime made of weapons. The
smith is a wide thing with too many arms, each working a different piece, and it does not
look up. //Steel keeps its promises,// it says, to no one. //Most of what's down here
doesn't. Buy accordingly.//
<</nobr>>

Up close the stock sorts itself: the honest iron, plain and fair-priced and dull with
use — and, scattered among it, a few pieces too fine and too cheap, a sword that should
cost a fortune marked at the price of a knife, gleaming where the others are grey. Coin
only, at this stall; no soul-trades, no foolish bargains, the smith having no use for the
other currency. Some of the stock is a great deal cheaper than it looks, and the smith
does not explain why.

It is the most workmanlike corner of the whole fair — no barker, no patter, no jingle,
just the hammer falling and falling on iron — and that plainness is its own small mercy
after the midway's roar, until you remember that a smith down here forges for a clientele,
and the clientele is the carnival, and you are wondering, now, what the too-cheap blades
were last used to do.

<div class="shop">
<div class="shop-head">Weapons</div>
<<for _e range setup.MARKET_WEAPONS>>
<<capture _e>>
<<set _blbl to "Buy " + setup.itemDisplayName(_e.id) + " (" + _e.price + " coins)">>
<div class="shop-row">
<<if setup.has(_e.id)>><span class="shop-have"><<= setup.itemDisplayName(_e.id)>> — owned</span>
<<elseif $coins gte _e.price>><<link _blbl>><<run setup.buy(_e.id, _e.price)>><<goto "TheSmithStall">><</link>> <span class="es-brief">(<<= setup.itemBrief(_e.id)>>)</span>
<<else>><span class="shop-cant"><<= setup.itemDisplayName(_e.id)>> — <<= _e.price>> coins</span> <span class="es-brief">(<<= setup.itemBrief(_e.id)>>)</span><</if>>
</div>
<</capture>>
<</for>>
<div class="shop-head">Armour</div>
<<for _e range setup.MARKET_ARMOR>>
<<capture _e>>
<<set _blbl to "Buy " + setup.itemDisplayName(_e.id) + " (" + _e.price + " coins)">>
<div class="shop-row">
<<if setup.has(_e.id)>><span class="shop-have"><<= setup.itemDisplayName(_e.id)>> — owned</span>
<<elseif $coins gte _e.price>><<link _blbl>><<run setup.buy(_e.id, _e.price)>><<goto "TheSmithStall">><</link>> <span class="es-brief">(<<= setup.itemBrief(_e.id)>>)</span>
<<else>><span class="shop-cant"><<= setup.itemDisplayName(_e.id)>> — <<= _e.price>> coins</span> <span class="es-brief">(<<= setup.itemBrief(_e.id)>>)</span><</if>>
</div>
<</capture>>
<</for>>
</div>

<div class="shop-coins">You have <<= $coins>> coin<<if $coins neq 1>>s<</if>>.</div>
<<link "Back to the market">><<goto "HellMarket">><</link>>


:: TheTrinketStall [carnival]
/* Accessories (setup.MARKET_TRINKETS) + an appraiser (setup.appraise) that identifies one
   unidentified pack item for coin. Cheap cursed bargains are masked the same as drops. */
<<if setup.isSimple()>>
<<nobr>>
The table is covered in shiny things. Rings and chains and bright little bits, all of them
catching the light and throwing it back pretty. You like them. You can tell you like them by the
way your hand has come up on its own, reaching, before you told it to.

The seller has a lot of rings on a lot of fingers and the idea of a smile, and it watches your
face do the thing your face is doing, and the smile spreads. //Ohh,// it says, slow and warm, the
way you would talk to a child stopped at a sweet-shop window. //You //like// the shiny ones.
'Course you do. Pretty things for —// a little pause, kindly — //well. 'Course you do. Try one
on.// It does not trouble, with you, to hide that some of the shiny things bite. There would be no
point: you could not follow the warning, and a warning you cannot follow is only more noise.
<</nobr>>

On its stool beside the table the one-eyed appraiser watches this, and lets a long breath out
through its eye, and does not say the thing it is plainly thinking — that there is no use selling
the truth of a thing to a soul who could neither count the coin to buy it nor keep the answer
once it was given.
<<else>>
<<nobr>>
The trinket-monger's table glitters in the smoke-light like a tide-pool of small bright
wrongness — rings and charms and chains and brooches and boots laid out on stained velvet,
every piece catching the carnival glow and throwing it back a colour it has no business
being. The monger has no face to speak of, only a great many rings on a great many fingers,
and a smile somewhere in the idea of it. //Try them on,// it purrs. //You can always try
them on. Taking them off again — well. That's a different stall.//
<</nobr>>

Lean in over the velvet and the glitter resolves into a hundred small histories: a ring worn
thin to a thread, a charm with somebody's initial half-rubbed off, boots scuffed by feet that
are not here to wear them now. Some of the pieces are plainly what they look like. Some are
priced too sweetly to be, the bargain sitting on the velvet like bait — and there is no telling
which from which by looking, which is, of course, the entire point of the table.

That is what the one-eyed appraiser is for, set up beside the table on a stool of its own, ready
to take a coin or six and tell you what a thing //truly// is — the only honest service in the
Shambles, and the monger lets it stand right there at its elbow, smiling its idea of a smile,
because a mark who learns the curse before buying is a mark who still, so very often, buys it
anyway.
<</if>>

<div class="shop">
<div class="shop-head">Charms & accessories</div>
<<for _e range setup.MARKET_TRINKETS>>
<<capture _e>>
<<set _blbl to "Buy " + setup.itemDisplayName(_e.id) + " (" + _e.price + " coins)">>
<div class="shop-row">
<<if setup.has(_e.id)>><span class="shop-have"><<= setup.itemDisplayName(_e.id)>> — owned</span>
<<elseif $coins gte _e.price>><<link _blbl>><<run setup.buy(_e.id, _e.price)>><<goto "TheTrinketStall">><</link>> <span class="es-brief">(<<= setup.itemBrief(_e.id)>>)</span>
<<else>><span class="shop-cant"><<= setup.itemDisplayName(_e.id)>> — <<= _e.price>> coins</span> <span class="es-brief">(<<= setup.itemBrief(_e.id)>>)</span><</if>>
</div>
<</capture>>
<</for>>
</div>

<<set _un to setup.unidentifiedItems()>>
<<if _un.length>>
<div class="shop">
<div class="shop-head">The appraiser (<<= setup.APPRAISE_COST>> coins each)</div>
<<for _id range _un>>
<<capture _id>>
<<set _albl to "Appraise " + setup.itemDisplayName(_id)>>
<div class="shop-row">
<<if $coins gte setup.APPRAISE_COST>><<link _albl>><<run setup.appraise(_id)>><<goto "TheTrinketStall">><</link>>
<<else>><span class="shop-cant"><<= setup.itemDisplayName(_id)>> — needs <<= setup.APPRAISE_COST>> coins</span><</if>>
</div>
<</capture>>
<</for>>
</div>
<</if>>

<div class="shop-coins">You have <<= $coins>> coin<<if $coins neq 1>>s<</if>>.</div>
<<link "Manage your gear">><<set $gearReturn to "TheTrinketStall">><<goto "Equipment">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the market">><<goto "HellMarket">><</link>>


:: HellTavern [carnival]
<<roomart "HellmouthInnOutside">>
The Hellmouth Inn keeps no walls worth the name — a long open-air bar thrown up under
a leaning roof at the very lip of a fire-arch, where a thread of the mouth's own flame
pours out across the cobbles and never once goes cold. Demons hunch along the rail on
low stools, easy as men after a shift; a castle's black teeth stand against the burning
sky behind them. Over the door hangs the sign.
<<roomart "HellmouthInnLogo">>
A burning mouth ringed with faces — the grinning and the damned crowded shoulder to
shoulder around the same hole — and under it, in a hand that tried for cheerful, **the
Hellmouth Inn**. Up close it is loud and warm and almost — almost — like one above:
a fire, a bar, patrons hunched over cups, an entertainer in the corner doing something
with its own ribcage that draws scattered applause. You could almost forget where you
are, which is of course exactly what the place is for. A barkeep slides you a cup you do
not drink — a big soft-spoken fellow in a stained apron you find you'd sooner not look at
head-on, something wrong about his eyes, or about how many of them seem to be on you at once.

<<linkreplace "Drink the cup the barkeep slid you, just this once">>
<<corrupt "torso" "pig" 6>>
You drink. It is warm and it is good and it tastes of the tavern itself, of somewhere-you-belong,
and it goes down easy and stays down, and a little of the room takes up quiet residence in you,
glad to be home.
<</linkreplace>>

Behind the free cup the barkeep slides every soul that stops here, the taps pour something the
house actually charges for — a cloudy, low, evil-smelling measure the regulars call //sewer-brew//,
drawn (they say) off a still somewhere down in the wet, where the deep cooks its own water into
something a body will pay to swallow. It is not for belonging. It is for the nerve it puts in a gut
that hasn't any, and the way it makes the floor stop tilting for a while; and the men hunched over it
have the look of men who came in for the one and are three years into the barrel.
<<if $coins gte setup.SEWERBREW_PRICE>>
<<linkreplace "Buy a measure of sewer-brew (3 coins)">>
<<set $coins to $coins - setup.SEWERBREW_PRICE>><<set _b to setup.drinkBrew()>><<nobr>>
You put your coin down on the wet wood and drink the thing off. It is vile, and it is warm, and it goes
off in your chest like a small brown sun — and for the length of that warmth the nerve comes back into
you and the room holds level and you understand exactly why a man would sell his back for the next one.
Then something oily and patient takes the moment's measure of you in return, and settles in where the
nerve had been.
//(+<<= _b.heal>> vigor<<if _b.mana gt 0>>, +<<= _b.mana>> mana<</if>>; <<= _b.bite>> filth worked into your gut, and the want of it set a notch deeper)//
<</nobr>><</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
//(a measure costs <<= setup.SEWERBREW_PRICE>> coin, and your purse is too light for even the one — this time.)//
<</if>>

The barkeep doesn't move on to the next patron. He stays in front of you, polishing a glass
that is already clean, all that wrong attention angled your way, plainly willing — plainly
//wanting// — to talk.
<<link "Look at him straight, and have a word">><<goto "TheFlyOnTheWall">><</link>>

The barkeep tips his head at the taps and the crowded room, and the offer is plain
enough without a mouth to make it: there is always a turn to be had behind the bar. How hard you
work it — and how much of yourself you let the room have — is your own affair, and the coin scales,
the way coin always does, with what you're willing to spend to earn it.

<<linkreplace "Keep your head down — pour, serve, say little">>
<<set _seg to setup.SEGMENTS.random()>><<set _amt to random(2, 4)>><<corrupt _seg "pig" _amt>>
<<set _pay to random(3, 5)>><<set _got to setup.gainCoins(_pay)>>
You work the quiet end of the bar with your eyes down, pulling the warm cup and sliding it on
without catching a single gaze, and the shift passes over you light — <<if _got gt 0>><<= _got>> coin<<if _got neq 1>>s<</if>><<else>>coin that curdles to rubbish in your grip before you can pocket it<</if>>
and only a little of the room soaking in where you weren't watching. Honest work, near enough; the
//near enough// is the part that costs.
<</linkreplace>>

<<linkreplace "Work the difficult ones — the regulars nobody else will serve">>
<<set _seg to setup.SEGMENTS.random()>><<set _amt to random(4, 7)>><<corrupt _seg "pig" _amt>>
<<set _pay to random(7, 11)>><<set _got to setup.gainCoins(_pay)>>
There are customers the other hands leave alone — the ones that want the cup poured a particular
wrong way, that lean in too close and ask you things, that tip well precisely because they know
what the closeness costs you. You serve them, and you are good at it, and you come away
<<if _got gt 0>><<= _got>> coin<<if _got neq 1>>s<</if>> richer<<else>>with a fist of coin that rots to rubbish before you can spend it<</if>> and wearing more of the room than you'd like, the
belonging gone a little deeper in for every regular you learned to keep happy.
<</linkreplace>>

<<linkreplace "Drink with the patrons — stop serving them and start being one">>
<<set _amt to random(9, 14)>><<corrupt "torso" "pig" _amt>>
<<set _pay to random(12, 18)>><<set _got to setup.gainCoins(_pay)>>
You come round the bar and sit down on the wrong side of it and drink with them, cup for cup, and
it is the easiest and warmest thing you have done since the sack came down — and the worst, because
they //love// you for it, fold you into the round like you were always theirs, and tip like family,
because for an hour you were. You pocket <<if _got gt 0>><<= _got>> coin<<if _got neq 1>>s<</if>><<else>>a fistful that curdles to rubbish in your pocket<</if>> and a great warm
slug of the tavern besides, and when you stand the floor is a half-step further off than it was, and
you are a half-step further in.
<</linkreplace>>

<<if setup.contestOffered()>>
Down the rail a big bloated thing with the still's own reek baked into its apron has been watching you
drink, and now it slaps the wet wood and shows you a grin with too many teeth in it and pushes a full
measure across to sit beside yours. It doesn't need a mouth to make the wager plain: cup for cup, you and
it, and the coin of every soul at the rail riding on whoever's still sitting up at the end. They think
they know how this goes — the mark never holds his drink. They are already laughing, already counting it.
<<link "Take the wager — match it cup for cup for coin">><<goto "TheDrinkingContest">><</link>>
<</if>>

In the back, where the floor is sticky and the light is bad, a demon rat the size
of a dog is worrying at a heap of scraps, and a circle of patrons is laying bets.
//Coin for anyone who'll put the vermin down,// the barkeep says, without a mouth.
//They breed in the walls. We're none of us fond of them.//

<<scentof "rat">>
<<link "Take the fight — put down the rat for coin">>
  <<run setup.startFight("rat", "HellTavern")>>
  <<goto "Combat">>
<</link>>
<<link "Slip down a back alley to the market">><<goto "TheBackAlleys">><</link>>
<<link "Out to the midway">><<goto "TheMidway">><</link>>


/* ---------- THE FLY ON THE WALL (the barkeep, up close — info broker) ---------- */

:: TheFlyOnTheWall [carnival]
<<roomart "flybartender">>
<<if setup.isSimple()>>
You look straight at the barkeep and the barkeep is a fly — a big one, all eyes — and your
mouth comes open looking at it and does not, quite, close again.

//Oh,// he says, and it arrives behind your ears, and it is gentle, and the gentleness is the
worst thing in the room. //Oh, friend. Right down to the floor, you've gone.// He sets his glass
aside slow, the way a man moves slow near a thing that spooks easy. //Keep your coin. There's no
trade to be had with what's left of you, and I haven't the heart to take it for nothing.//

He leans on his folded hands and talks small, because small is the size of what you can hold now.
//The big mouth. That you were carried in by. The marks cut over it.// He waits, after each piece,
to watch it land. //They are //words.// You could //learn// them. Go and stand under them — that's
all — go and stand under the words.// Something that is almost sorrow crosses a few hundred facets
at once, because he can see, even as he says it, that it has already gone out of you like water
through a grate. //...Aye. Never mind, friend. Drink your drink. The drink's the part that stays.//

<<link "Drink the drink, and go">><<goto "HellTavern">><</link>>
<<else>>
You let yourself look straight at the barkeep at last, and the barkeep looks back with
everything he has. The face you'd been not-looking-at is a fly's: two great wet domes of
eye, each one a thousand smaller eyes, every facet holding its own little copy of you and
the fire and the room. He sets down the glass he is forever polishing, folds two delicate
hands on the marble, and when he speaks it is without a mouth — a sound that arrives behind
your ears rather than in them.

//I am the fly on the wall,// he says, not unkindly. //Every wall. I have sat on all of
them, in this place and the deeper ones, and a fly sees a great deal that reckons itself
unwatched.// He sets the clean glass down in front of you and folds two delicate hands, in
no hurry at all. //But I am a barkeep before I am an oracle, friend, and a barkeep talks to
a man who's bought something and left a little for the help. Stand us a round; leave a coin
on the bar for the fly. Then we're friends — and friends talk.//

<<if $flyTipped>>
You have stood him his round and left a coin under the saucer, and it has bought you the
full warm weight of his attention: he leans in on his folded hands, friendly now, and waits
for the question.

<<if not $heardOfGrimoire>>
<<linkreplace "Ask what's worth knowing down the stall row">>
<<set $heardOfGrimoire to true>>
//The privy,// he says, and a ripple of amusement crosses a few hundred facets at once.
//The shut stall, the one nobody is ever in — the one with the pink light coming up out of
the hole like a sunrise pointed the wrong way. There's a book in there. //Was// a book. It's
bog-roll now, but the words are still good words, the kind a person could //learn// from, if
a person could catch the stall open and could read what's printed on it. Two of the regulars
were on about it not an hour past, right where you're sitting.//
<</linkreplace>>
<</if>>

<<if not $heardOfWord>>
<<linkreplace "Ask about the mouth you were carried in through">>
<<set $heardOfWord to true>>
//The arch,// he says. //All those names cut into the stone over the throat. Everyone takes
them for a tally — the roll of every poor soul the mouth has ever swallowed. And it //is//
that. But a list is a thing that was //written//, friend, and a thing that was written can be
//read//, and reading it the right way is the oldest trick there is down here. The daylight
never taught you a word like that one. The dark will, if you go back and stand under it like
you mean to.//
<</linkreplace>>
<</if>>

<<linkreplace "Ask what he sees when he looks at you">>
A thousand small copies of you turn the question over at once.
<<if setup.isDim()>>
//I see there's less of you home than there was,// he says, gentle as anything, and means it
kindly, which is the worse for it. //No judgement in it — but you came in for the talking,
friend, and the talking's mostly wasted on you now; it goes in and finds no one to keep it.
That's the cruel little mercy of going simple: you won't fret the loss, because fretting's the
first thing to go. Drink your drink. The drink still lands.//
<<else>>
//I see how much of the room you've got on you already,// he says, gentle as anything. //It's
no judgement — we've all got the room on us; that's what a room like this is //for//. I only
mean: you came in to be served, and you're halfway to being a regular, and you never felt the
turn. Nobody ever does. That is the one good use of a fly on the wall — to tell you the wall is
nearer than it looks.//
<</if>>
<</linkreplace>>

<<else>>

<<if $coins gte 2>>
He waits, all those eyes patient and pleasant, until you do the small expected thing.
<<link "Buy a round, and tuck a coin under his saucer for the fly">>
<<set $coins to $coins - 2>><<set $flyTipped to true>><<corrupt "torso" "pig" 2>>
<<goto "TheFlyOnTheWall">>
<</link>>
<<else>>
He waits, pleasant and patient, until it becomes plain you have nothing on you to spend —
and then, not unkindly, he turns back to polishing his glass. A fly keeps his walls for
paying friends; come back with a coin or two in your pocket and he will talk.
<</if>>

<</if>>

<<link "Thank him, and turn back to the bar">><<goto "HellTavern">><</link>>
<</if>>


/* ---------- THE DRINKING CONTEST (the sewer-brew con — owner 2026-07-20) ----------
   An EASY con check with an INVERTED payoff: win and you drank a whole contest to do it (purse
   + the big dose, brew rules you fastest); lose and you only had the one measure (no coin, a
   smaller forfeit). The better you hold your drink, the harder you are hooked — the coin is the
   bait. setup.drinkingContest() does the check + both doses (setup.drinkBrew) + the purse. EV
   verified a sucker's game, not a coin faucet (docs/COMBAT.md "The drinking contest"). */

:: TheDrinkingContest [carnival]
<<set _c to setup.drinkingContest()>>
<<if _c.won>>
You match it. You match it cup for cup and then a cup past that, and somewhere in the back half of the
barrel the rail stops laughing and starts watching, and then starts betting the other way — and the big
bloated thing in the apron slides slowly sideways off its stool and does not get up, and you are, barely,
swaying, the fire-arch smeared to one long orange streak across your eye, the last one sitting. The rail
pays out. Hands you have never met clap your back. You won.

//But you drank a whole contest to win it.// You come away <<if _c.prize gt 0>><<= _c.prize>> coin<<if _c.prize neq 1>>s<</if>> the richer<<else>>clutching a fist of winnings that curdles to rubbish before it can warm your palm<</if>> — and carrying the barrel out with you. The nerve of it first, and then the sick brown weight under the nerve, and the taste that will not leave, and the //want//, now, gone in one night from a notch to a hook set deep.
<<nobr>>
//(+<<= _c.heal>> vigor<<if _c.mana gt 0>>, +<<= _c.mana>> mana<</if>>; <<= _c.bite>> filth worked deep into your gut, and the brew's hold set hard<<if setup.dominantAddiction() is "brew">> — it is the loudest thing in you now<<else>> — though the cheese still calls the louder<</if>>)//
<</nobr>>
<<else>>
You match it for a cup, and a second, and then the floor comes up at an angle floors are not meant to,
and the room folds itself in half, and the last thing you are sure of is that wet grin across the rail
not slowing down by so much as a swallow. When the tilt finally lets go of you the wager is long lost,
the rail has pocketed its coin and its laughter both, and the bloated thing is gone off to find an easier
mark. You had the one measure — well, two — and you have nothing to show for them but the sour start of a
thirst you did not walk in with.
<<nobr>>
//(no coin — you lost. +<<= _c.heal>> vigor<<if _c.mana gt 0>>, +<<= _c.mana>> mana<</if>>; <<= _c.bite>> filth into your gut, and the want of it set a notch deeper)//
<</nobr>>
<</if>>

<<link "Peel yourself off the rail and go back to the bar">><<goto "HellTavern">><</link>>


/* ---------- THE BACK ALLEYS (market <-> tavern, per the bible) ---------- */

:: TheBackAlleys [carnival]
Behind the lit stalls the Shambles show their working: a warren of back alleys
where the carnival keeps what it doesn't put on display — the crates and the slop
and the off-duty barkers smoking something that glows, the seams and stitches of the
fairground, the place the fun is assembled and the place it is, afterward, swept up.
The alleys link the market and the tavern the short way, away from the midway's
press, and they are quieter, and the quiet is worse, because out here you can hear
the carnival the way you hear a slaughterhouse from the car park: as a sound a happy
place should not, underneath, be making.

<<linkreplace "Share the off-duty barkers' glowing smoke">><<nobr>>
<<set _r to setup.drawSmoke()>>
You take the offered draw because it would be rude not to, and the off-duty barkers grin and make room,
colleagues already. The glow is cool going down, pale and slow, nothing like a fire — a marsh-light you can
breathe — and it does not go to your head so much as to your //edges//, which soften. The ache eases. The
part of you that was minding all this eases too, and does not entirely come back, gone out thin with the
smoke to hang in the room with everyone else's, so that for a moment you cannot say where you stop and the
shift begins, and it is a warm and colleague-ly feeling and you do not mind it, which is the thing you
should have minded.
//(the glow eases you and diffuses you: +<<= _r.heal>> vigor, <<= _r.total>> of you spread thin across <<= _r.n>> parts — no limb turns, you just go a little less //one thing//)//
<</nobr>><</linkreplace>>

<<if not $alleyHand>>
One of the off-duty barkers tips his glowing smoke at you, friendly, colleague to colleague, and seems
to want a word more than to share a light.
<<linkreplace "Hear out the off-duty barker">><<nobr>><<set $alleyHand to true>>
//Don't mind us — this is just where they build it, and where they sweep it up after. Every fair's got
a back of house, and this is ours.// He draws on the glow without looking at it. //I came down a mark,
same as you. Owed a night's keep, so I took a night barking a stall. Just the one. You only ever take
the one.// A shrug, easy, professional. //Now I'm on the rota. Couldn't tell you which half of what I
say is still me and which half's the patter — stopped being able to tell, is the thing. You stop
minding, and then you stop being the one who'd have minded.// He nods toward the wet stair behind the
market, where the off-cuts go. //Down's where it all ends up, anyhow. No hurry.//
<</nobr>><</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
He's back at the smoke, working a knot out of the next stall's patter; he gives you a colleague's nod
and nothing more — the shift doesn't talk twice.
<</if>>

<<link "Through to the market">><<goto "HellMarket">><</link>>
<<link "Through to the tavern">><<goto "HellTavern">><</link>>
<<link "Past a confectioner's cart">><<goto "TheConfectioner">><</link>>
<<link "Down the wet stair, where the off-cuts go">><<goto "TheStill">><</link>>


/* ---------- THE STILL (the filth JOB — owner 2026-07-20) ----------
   The distillery that makes the sewer-brew (the tavern's bought filth drink). Pairs the filth
   FOOD with its LABOR — grueling, poorly paid in coin, and paying its wages in the DRINK
   (payment-in-kind: work the vice's source, get hooked on the vice). setup.stillShift(mode) does
   the corruption/coin/ration; the in-kind wage feeds the brew addiction (dominance → withdrawal).
   Seeded by the TheBackAlleys alley-hand's "down's where it all ends up". */

:: TheStill [carnival]
Down the wet stair the noise of the fair thins to nothing and a different sound takes over: a slow
tarry //drip//, and under it a low animal seethe, like a pot forgetting it was ever water. This is
where the sewer is cooked. Vast bellied vats sweat along the walls, fed by pipes that come straight up
out of the muck, and the deep's own filth turns over in them slow and brown and, God help you, //ripe//
— and somewhere in the heat a thin bright thread of it is running off a coil into a jar, clear as guilt,
which is the brew the taps upstairs pour warm to men who came in for the one. The stillmen work it
half-sunk and unhurried, big bloated things gone the colour of what they tend, and the reek is a
physical weight, and it is not, quite, entirely awful, and the //not-quite// is the part that costs.

<<if not $stillmanHeard>>
One of the stillmen straightens off a vat and considers you with the patience of a thing that has
watched a great many marks come down this stair.
<<linkreplace "Hear the stillman out">><<nobr>><<set $stillmanHeard to true>>
//Work's honest enough,// it says, and its voice comes up bubbling. //Hard, and wet, and it don't pay
much — coin's for them upstairs, the market and the wheel; down here we're where the money //isn't//.//
It tips the running jar, fond. //But we never run short of the //one// thing. So we pay a man in what we
//make//. Take your night's keep in the drink and you'll not go without — that's the offer, and it's a
fair one, and//— it shows you a wet grey grin —//every last one of us took it. You start taking your wage
in the barrel, and one day you look up and you're the barrel, and you don't mind, because the barrel
doesn't.//
<</nobr>><</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
The stillman gives you the nod of a thing you work beside now, and turns back to the coil; the shift
doesn't say it twice.
<</if>>

There is work here for anyone with a strong enough stomach and few enough choices. What you take for it
is your own affair.

<<linkreplace "Carry water to the mash — light work, keep your head up out of the reek">><<nobr>>
<<set _s to setup.stillShift("light")>>
You haul and pour and keep your face turned from the worst of it, and it is honest and it is nothing, and
you come away <<if _s.coin gt 0>><<= _s.coin>> coin<<if _s.coin neq 1>>s<</if>> the better<<else>>with a coin that curdles to rubbish before you can pocket it<</if>> and only a little of the wet soaked into you where you weren't watching.
//(<<= _s.filth>> filth worked into you; <<if _s.coin gt 0>>+<<= _s.coin>> coin<<else>>no coin kept<</if>>)//
<</nobr>><</linkreplace>>

<<linkreplace "Work the fumes — draw the spirit off the mash, down in the heat">><<nobr>>
<<set _s to setup.stillShift("hard")>>
You go down into the heat where the coil runs and you breathe what comes off the mash for hours, and it
gets into you the way weather gets into a wall, and you come up <<if _s.coin gt 0>><<= _s.coin>> coin<<if _s.coin neq 1>>s<</if>> the better<<else>>with a fist of coin that rots to rubbish before you can spend it<</if>> and wearing more of the still than you walked in with.
//(<<= _s.filth>> filth worked deep into you; <<if _s.coin gt 0>>+<<= _s.coin>> coin<<else>>no coin kept<</if>>)//
<</nobr>><</linkreplace>>

<<linkreplace "Take your wage in the drink — coin's for them upstairs">><<nobr>>
<<set _s to setup.stillShift("inkind")>>
You work the shift and take your keep the way the stillmen do — in the jar, straight off the coil, still
warm from the mash. It goes down evil and easy and puts the nerve back in you, and it is the //best//
part of the day and you understand, drinking it, exactly why not one of them ever left. You pocket
<<if _s.coin gt 0>>a single coin besides<<else>>no coin — there wasn't any, there never is<</if>>, and carry the rest of your wage out inside you.
//(the work: <<= _s.filth>> filth into you. the wage: +<<= _s.ration.heal>> vigor<<if _s.ration.mana gt 0>>, +<<= _s.ration.mana>> mana<</if>>; <<= _s.ration.bite>> filth into your gut, and the brew's hook set <<if setup.dominantAddiction() is "brew">>hard — it is the loudest thing in you now<<else>>deeper<</if>>)//
<</nobr>><</linkreplace>>

<<link "Back up the wet stair to the alleys">><<goto "TheBackAlleys">><</link>>
<<link "Follow the fumes the other way, where the air goes to gather">><<goto "TheGuttering">><</link>>


/* ---------- THE GUTTERING (the smoke creatures' den — owner 2026-07-20; Phase 0 of the FOURTH class,
   docs/HANDOFF-SMOKE-CREATURES.md) ----------
   Past the still, the dead-end where the cooked-off vapour and the fair's swept-up smoke and the
   marsh-light off the muck all pool and thicken — the "off-cuts of the air". Here the aggregate stands
   up: the SHADOW-PERSON (a person-shape of smoke, soot, and flies, speaking as WE) trailing SMOKE-WISPS
   (ignis fatuus, the glow that says this-way). Their combat signature is IDENTITY DAMAGE (idmg) — they
   barely tear flesh but every touch takes a little of what you ARE; the chorus move takes from several
   segments at once (a miniature of the un-built Phase-3 plurality horror). Built class FILTH for now.
   Payoff-tie: this is also a faucet for the smoke VICE (draw the glow here). You cannot KILL smoke, only
   SCATTER it — the cleared lander says so (it thins for now; the haze, and the vice, remain). Grounded:
   ignis fatuus = luminous swamp-gas off decomposition (cool/pale, mechanism genuinely disputed — no NPC
   "explains" it); the "we" = siphonophore/colonial-organism biology (many zooids, one animal). */

:: TheGuttering [carnival]
<<roomart "guttering">>
Past the still the passage does not end so much as //thin//. The cooked-off vapour from the vats and the
swept-up smoke of the fair and something colder that rises straight off the muck all come here to pool,
because air has its low places same as water, and this is one. It hangs waist-deep and slow, and here and
there a pale light moves in it — not a fire, nothing so warm; a cold marsh-glow, the kind that sits over a
bog or a fresh grave, that drifts off when you look at it and is somewhere else when you look back.

You have been thinking of the smoke as //weather//. It is not weather. Where it pools thickest it has
gathered itself up on end, and it is standing, and it is shaped — near enough a person, held in an outline
out of habit, coming apart a little at every edge and drawing itself back. It notices you. When it speaks it
speaks with more than one voice, none of them quite in time, and what it says is //we//.

<<if $gutteringCleared>>
The shape you scattered has not reformed — not here, not yet — but the haze it was has not gone anywhere
either; it hangs where it always hangs, waiting for enough of itself. A pale wisp or two still drift the low
air, incurious now. You can breathe it, if the glow is what you have come to want. Nothing here will stop
you.

<<if setup.craving() is "smoke">>
<<linkreplace "Draw a long pull of the pooled glow">><<nobr>>
<<set _r to setup.drawSmoke()>>
You breathe it deep, here at the source, and the ache eases and the fear eases and a little more of you goes
out with the exhale to hang in the room with the rest, and does not come back, and you do not mind, which is
how you know it is working.
//(+<<= _r.heal>> vigor; <<= _r.total>> of you diffused thin across <<= _r.n>> parts — no limb turns, you just go vaguer)//
<</nobr>><</linkreplace>>
<</if>>

<<link "Back to the still">><<goto "TheStill">><</link>>
<<else>>
<<linkreplace "Draw a pull of the glow it offers — colleague to colleague">><<nobr>>
<<set _r to setup.drawSmoke()>>
It is not all threat. It moves the pale light toward you the way the barkers pass their smoke, //share-and-share//,
and you take it because it would be rude not to, and the ache eases, and the part of you that was afraid eases,
and thins, and joins the rest of it hanging in the air. //We//, it says, pleased, as if you have agreed to
something.
//(+<<= _r.heal>> vigor; <<= _r.total>> of you spread thin across <<= _r.n>> parts)//
<</nobr>><</linkreplace>>

The shape waits, patient as weather, for you to stop being one thing. You can put it off — scatter it, and buy
yourself the walk back up the stair still singular — or you can turn and go while you are still the one turning.

<<link "Scatter it — swing through the shape before it draws you in">>
<<run setup.startFight(["shadowperson", "smokewisp", "smokewisp"], "TheGutteringCleared")>>
<<goto "Combat">>
<</link>>
<<link "Back to the still while you are still the one deciding">><<goto "TheStill">><</link>>
<</if>>

:: TheGutteringCleared [carnival]
<<set $gutteringCleared to true>>
<<nobr>>
You swing, and there is a sick half-second where there is nothing to swing //at// — it has come apart around
the blow — and then your club (or your fist, or the flat of whatever you carry) finds the density at the
core of it, the soot and the wet and the drone of flies that was holding the person-shape together, and
//scatters// it. The outline blows out like breath on a cold morning. The voices go out of the air one at a
time, unhurried even now, and the last of them is still saying //we// as it thins to nothing.
<</nobr>>

<<set _t to setup.scatterToll()>>
But you cannot put smoke anywhere except the air, and the air is the air you are breathing. Scattering it,
you scatter it //into// you — a lungful of the person it was, warm and greasy and going down without asking —
and standing there getting your breath in the settling haze you take a little of it in with every gasp, and a
little of you goes vague to make the room.
//(you breathe it scattering: <<= _t.total>> of it worked thin into <<= _t.n>> parts of you — the toll of winning; you cannot fight smoke without breathing it)//

You have not killed it. You know that, standing in the settling haze — you cannot kill smoke, you can only
give it back to the room, and the room is patient. But it will not stand up again today, and today is what
you have.

<<link "Go on, before it remembers how to be a person">><<goto "TheStill">><</link>>


/* ---------- THE BIG TOP + ATTRACTIONS ---------- */

:: TheBigTop [carnival]
The big top stands at the heart of the midway — a tent the size of a cathedral, its
canvas the grey-pink of something tanned rather than woven, swelling and subsiding
gently as though the whole tent breathes. From the foot of it you have to tip your head
back to find the top, where the great centre-pole vanishes up into smoke, and the whole
vast skin of it pulses, slow and warm, in and out, in and out, the way a thing pulses
that is alive and at rest and very large.

Step under the flap and the dark inside is total for a moment and then resolves wrong:
there is a ring, and a roar, and a crowd you can't quite see — you feel them more than
find them, a tiered weight of attention banked up into the dark on every side, delighting
at a spectacle in the centre you also can't quite make out, only its edges, only the way
the light bends around whatever is performing there. The breathing you took for the tent's
is the crowd's, you understand, all of them, in and out together.

And over it all rolls a ringmaster's voice, the most dangerous voice in the Shambles, the
words that have undone everyone who came this far before you — //step inside, step inside,
you've come so far, you've earned a little wonder.// And you have come far. And you are so
tired. And a little wonder sounds, just now, like exactly the thing.

<<linkreplace "Step inside. Watch a little of the show.">>
<<corrupt "head" "pig" 4>>
You step in, just to watch, just for a minute — and the ring and the roar and the crowd you can't
quite see take that minute and several after it, and when you make yourself leave you are humming
the calliope's wrong tune under your breath, and you cannot make yourself stop.
<</linkreplace>>

<<link "Backstage, where the acts wait">><<goto "TheStage">><</link>>
<<link "A sideshow tent — see the wonders within">><<goto "TheFreakShow">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the midway">><<goto "TheMidway">><</link>>


:: TheStage [carnival]
Backstage of the big top is a warm crowded dark behind the canvas, lit by the bleed of the
ring-lights through the cloth and smelling of greasepaint and sweat and something
underneath the greasepaint that the greasepaint is there to cover. This is where the acts
wait their turn, and the air is thick with the particular backstage hum of performers
between calls — limbering, muttering lines, helping each other into costume.

As your eyes adjust the performers come clear one by one, and each one is a small horror
worn easy: the entertainer from the tavern flexing its ribcage open and shut in warm-up,
a contortionist folding itself through a ring smaller than its skull, a troupe of the
boneless stretching in a heap. And all of them are companionable, professional, *kind* to
you — an unmistakable warmth among performers toward a newcomer they've taken for one of
their own. They make room. They ask which act is yours.

The assumption is the trap, gentle as all the Shambles' traps: that you are here to
perform, that you have a wonder in you, that you came down to join the show and simply
haven't picked your trick yet. Nobody asks how you got backstage. Nobody asks for a name.
They have simply decided you belong, and the awful pull of it is how badly, after the
long way down, a part of you would like to prove them right.

<<linkreplace "Try a turn — just to see if you've a wonder in you">>
<<corrupt "head" "pig" 4>>
You try a little something — a pose, a trick you didn't know you had — and the performers applaud
warmly and a part of you preens at it, pleased, //belonging//, and that part does not entirely sit
back down when the moment passes.
<</linkreplace>>

<<link "A barker waves you toward an empty cage — a paid turn as an oddity">><<goto "TheCageAct">><</link>>
<<link "Deeper backstage, to the gaff">><<goto "TheGaff">><</link>>
<<link "Back into the big top">><<goto "TheBigTop">><</link>>


:: TheGaff [carnival]
The gaff — carnival cant for the secret of a trick — is the room behind the room,
where the Shambles keep how it's done: the rigged wheels with their hidden brakes,
the two-way mirrors, the cold-reading scripts, the jars of what the freak-show's
wonders are grown from, the whole apparatus of a fairground that runs, like every
fairground, on knowing exactly what a tired and hopeful mark wants and selling it to
them at a markup. It should be reassuring, seeing the strings. It isn't. The strings
go somewhere, and you follow one with your eye, and it goes into the dark overhead and
does not, anywhere you can see, come down.

<<linkreplace "Follow the string up into the dark, as far as your eye will go">>
<<corrupt "head" "pig" 4>>
You follow the string up past the rigging into the dark where it never comes down, and the dark
notices you noticing and looks back, with an attention almost fond, and you feel yourself filed
somewhere, catalogued, //expected// — and you bring your eyes down fast, and they bring a little of
the dark down with them.
<</linkreplace>>

<<if not $knowsTheGaff>>
A grease-handed mechanic tends the rigging back here, and clocks you as someone who has seen the
strings — and offers, one professional to a promising amateur, to let you in on the trade properly: a
worker's eye, a knack for never being the mark again. The apparatus only trusts what it has made part
of itself, it says. It would cost you a real piece of you. But you would never be played again.
<<link "Let it show you the working — become staff, near enough">>
<<set $knowsTheGaff to true>><<corrupt "armR" "pig" 25>>
<<goto "TheButchersStall">>
<</link>>
<<link "Keep your hands your own, and walk out a mark">>
<<soothe 6>>
<<goto "TheStage">>
<</link>>
<<else>>
The mechanic gives you the nod it gives the staff now — you're one of the working, near enough, and the
games will never quite fully take you for a mark again. It goes back to the rigging.

<<link "Out to the butcher's stall">><<goto "TheButchersStall">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the stage">><<goto "TheStage">><</link>>
<</if>>


/* ---------- GAMES + CACHES ---------- */

:: TheGames [carnival]
A row of games runs down one side of the midway, stall after lit stall, each one a barker
and a counter and a feat — ring the bell, hook the duck, guess which cup the soul is under
— and the whole row is a wall of noise and colour and invitation, the barkers' calls
overlapping into one long pleading sales-roar, *try it, try it, only a coin, anyone can
win.*

Walk the row and the feats sort themselves into the kind you can already feel are crooked:
the bell rigged too high, the ducks weighted, the cups quicker than any hand watching them.
They are all rigged, of course — that is the honest part of the carnival, the part that
plays fair about cheating. And hung overhead, swaying on their hooks above every stall, are
the prizes: gaudy, worthless, plush nothings in colours that don't occur, dangling just out
of reach.

And the trap is not the rigging — you can see the rigging — the trap is that you //want//
one. Some garish stuffed thing up there has caught your eye and will not let it go, and you
stand at the counter knowing exactly how the game is cheated and find, with a small cold
alarm, that you would still genuinely like to win it, that you are already reaching for a
coin to lose.

<<if setup.isDim()>>
A tout starts in on you — the lucky-one patter, the steered eye, the whole warm rigged routine — and you
watch his mouth make its shapes and do not follow where they are going, and he sees that you do not, and
the pitch dies in him halfway. He looks you over, recalculating, and jerks a thumb down the row at the
duck-pond and the ring-toss, the slow bright games they keep for children and the very drunk. //Those are
more your speed, friend,// he says, not unkindly. //No shame in it.// There is a little shame in it. You
are too far down, now, to be the one who collects it.
<<elseif not $wheelToutHeard>>
A barker between shows clocks you and starts talking like you're already partners — a tout, by the shine
of him, working the row for the wheel turning at its end.
<<linkreplace "Let the tout talk you toward the wheel">><<nobr>><<set $wheelToutHeard to true>>
//You've the look of a lucky one — I can always tell.// He steers your eye to the great wheel at the
row's end. //That wheel //pays,// friend, don't let the long faces fool you. I watched a man clear forty
on a single spin not an hour past and walk out of here a prince. Go on — put a coin down while the
luck's standing this close to you.// He does not mention the brake the barker's hand never leaves; he
does not mention what the //other// kind of bet — the one where you've no coin and stake //yourself// —
takes off a man when it loses. His own pockets, you notice, don't jingle. And he does not, himself, go
and spin it.
<</nobr>><</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
The tout's still working the row, still sure your luck's in, still steering every fresh face toward the
wheel he never plays. His pockets are as empty as when you met him.
<</if>>

<<link "A wheel of fortune, spinning slow">><<goto "TheWheel">><</link>>
<<link "A menagerie of caged attractions">><<goto "TheMenagerie">><</link>>
<<link "Off at the row's edge, a man throwing bones into the gutter">><<goto "TheGutterBones">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the midway">><<goto "TheMidway">><</link>>


:: TheWheel [carnival]
/* NOTE (builder): mechanics are in setup.spinWheel / setup.WHEEL (sewer-demons.twee);
   this prose is serviceable but PLACEHOLDER — owner intends to write the wheel-option
   copy. Outcomes: jackpot (big coins), win, push, lose, nick (pig corruption), and the
   curse / "asking for trouble" self-bet. Keep the kinds; rewrite the flavour freely. */
A great wheel of fortune turns slow at the row's end, its rim painted in prizes and
penalties, a barker's hand resting on the brake you are not supposed to notice.
//Round you go,// it says, //small stake, big return, everyone's a winner who plays
long enough.// It pays out just often enough to keep you turning it — that is the whole
trick, and knowing the trick has never once been enough to stop anybody.

<<if $wheelPlayed>>You know the wheel now, and the wheel knows you, and neither piece of
knowing has ever helped a soul step back from the felt.<</if>>

You have <span class="k-coins"><<= $coins>> coin<<if $coins neq 1>>s<</if>></span>.

<<if $coins gte setup.WHEEL_STAKE>>
<<linkreplace "Stake four coins and spin the wheel">>
<<set $coins to $coins - setup.WHEEL_STAKE>><<set $wheelPlayed to true>>
<<set _r to setup.spinWheel("coin")>>
<<switch _r.kind>>
<<case "jackpot">>The rim comes down on the painted sunburst at the very top — the prize
nobody believes is real until it is — and the barker counts out <<= _r.coins>> coins onto
the felt with a face that has gone, very slightly, sick.<<if _r.got lte 0>> You sweep them up and they curdle to rubbish in your fist — the curse on you eats the whole improbable win, and the barker's sick look eases into something like relief.<</if>> //Beginner's luck,// it manages.
//Quit while you're ahead, eh?// You should. You already know you won't.
<<case "win">>The wheel clicks down on a prize, and the barker pays <<= _r.coins>> coins
with a showman's flourish and a look that says //come back.//<<if _r.got lte 0>> The coins rot to rubbish in your palm as you gather them; the curse leaves you the flourish and nothing else.<</if>> You won. That is how it starts.
<<case "push">>The wheel clicks down on a blank and the barker rakes your stake off the
felt without quite meeting your eye. //Ooh. Go again? Win it back?// You lost the bet, and
nothing more — which is exactly how it keeps you spending the next one.
<<case "lose">>The wheel clicks down on a penalty, and the barker takes its due — not just
the coin it rakes off the felt, but the other thing, a small bright sliver of you palmed off
the rim as smoothly as a card. Where its shadow crossed you, something under the skin shifts,
and answers, and a little of you is the wheel's now.
<<case "curse">>The wheel clicks down on a mark you have no word for, and the barker leans in
quick and breathes something into your open mouth — a small wet syllable that settles under
your tongue and //stays//. <<if _r.alreadyCursed>>It nestles in beside the one already there,
and the two of them get comfortable.<<else>>//That one's on the house,// it grins. From now
on, every so often, your mouth will not say the thing you meant to say.<</if>>
<</switch>>
<</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
//(You haven't the four coins the wheel asks to play it straight.)//
<</if>>

<<linkreplace "Put yourself on the wheel instead — the barker calls it 'asking for trouble'">>
<<set $wheelPlayed to true>><<set _r to setup.spinWheel("trouble")>>
<<switch _r.kind>>
<<case "jackpot">>You stake the only thing you brought down here that's worth anything, and
the wheel — against every law of a rigged thing — comes down on the sunburst. <<= _r.coins>>
coins. The barker pays it like a man paying a ransom, fast and without joy, and the whole row
goes quiet watching, because the house is not supposed to lose the big one and everyone saw it.<<if _r.got lte 0>> Then the coins curdle to rubbish in your hands in front of all of them, the curse taking back what the house lost, and the row lets out a low ugly laugh.<</if>>
<<case "win">>The wheel throws you a bone — <<= _r.coins>> coins, a consolation that feels, for
one bright second, like getting away with something. You did not get away with anything.<<if _r.got lte 0>> The coins prove it, going to rubbish in your fist before you've closed it.<</if>>
<<case "lose">>The wheel takes a small bite — it never wanted your coin — just a little of you,
palmed off the rim as smoothly as a card. Where its shadow crossed you, something under the skin
answers, and goes over to the wheel's side of the felt.
<<case "transform">>The wheel takes a deep bite, and it is not coin it takes. Where the rim's shadow
falls across you the flesh answers it, eagerly, the way the whole carnival has been waiting for
you to let it — and a real part of you goes over to the wheel's side of the felt for good.
<<case "curse">>The wheel comes down on the sign with no name, and the barker breathes the small
wet word into your mouth. <<if _r.alreadyCursed>>It settles in beside its twin.<<else>>From now
on your mouth does not always say what you mean — and that, the barker's grin says, was always
the real game.<</if>>
<</switch>>
<</linkreplace>>

<<link "On, to a lost-and-found">><<goto "TheLostAndFound">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the games">><<goto "TheGames">><</link>>


:: TheGutterBones [carnival]
/* NOTE (builder): mechanics are setup.castBones / setup.BONES_TABLE (sewer-demons.twee).
   The chalked board below IS the published table — keep its numbers in sync with BONES_TABLE
   and the coin line with setup.BONES_PRICE (10). INT moves via $statMods; the witless floor
   is setup.WITLESS_INT (3). owner spec 2026-06-27. */
<<if $bonesThrown is 0>>
At the very edge of the row, past the last stall's light, a man is crouched over the
gutter-grate where all the carnival's runoff goes down. Not a stall. No canvas, no prizes,
no patter — just him, and a chalked square of kerb, and a cup of bones he tips and tips into
the muck.

The bones are old and yellow and have too many sides — knuckles off something that had
knuckles to spare. On the kerb beside them a price is chalked up plain as a butcher's board:
what each face takes, what each face gives. He does not look up at you. He does not call you
lucky. That is the new thing here, the thing the wheel's barker never managed — there is no
pitch, because the bones do not need one. The odds are posted. Read them and crouch down, or
don't.

And you read them, and understand them completely, and feel the same small cold pull you felt
under the dangling prizes — because the bones are honest about being a robbery, and being
robbed in the open is somehow worse, and because what they want is not in your pocket. They
want the thing that did the reading. Somewhere a careful part of you is already working the
sum. A louder part has crouched down without asking it.
<<else>>
You stay crouched at the kerb. The bone-man waits, patient as the grate.

<<if setup.isDim()>>
He looks at you now. He did not before — there was nothing in it for him then — but he looks at you now the way a man looks at a thing he has already won: mildly, briefly, without much interest, and goes back to tipping his cup.
<</if>>

<<if $bonesLast>>
<<if $bonesLast.coins gt 0>>
The bones come up on the eight. The bone-man counts ten coins out of his cup into your hand —
the first time he's looked at you — and something almost like a smile crosses him, and is gone.
<<if $bonesLast.got gt 0>>You won. You can feel, already, how much you would like to win again.<<else>>Then the coins curdle to rubbish in your palm — the curse on you eats them where you stand — and the bone-man's almost-smile widens, because a win you cannot keep is the best kind, for him.<</if>>
<<elseif $bonesLast.dint is -3>>
The bones come up on the one. The bone-man chalks a long mark on the kerb and three things go
out of you at once — you feel them go, like names you'll never get back — and the gutter takes
them down with the rest of the runoff. The world is, very slightly, simpler than it was. You do
not entirely notice. That is how you know it worked.
<<elseif $bonesLast.dint is -1>>
The bones come up low. The bone-man chalks a short mark, and a single small thing slides out of
you and down the grate — a word, a way you had of seeing — gone so quietly you have to take its
absence on faith. He tips the cup again. He has all night.
<<elseif $bonesLast.dint is 1>>
The bones come up kind. Something settles back into you, cool and clarifying, a little of
yourself returned — and having it back feels so good, so much like winning, that you have
already forgotten it was yours to begin with. Go again. You'll be ahead in no time.
<<elseif $bonesLast.dint is 3>>
The bones come up on the twelve. Three bright pieces of mind drop back into you at once and for
a moment you are sharper than you have been since they took you — sharp enough to see exactly
what this is, and sharp enough, therefore, to feel precisely how badly you want to throw again.
<<else>>
The bones come up in the middle. The bone-man chalks nothing, takes nothing, gives nothing,
tips the cup again. The gutter's patience is the whole machine: it does not need to win this
throw. It only needs you crouched here for the next one.
<</if>>
<</if>>
<</if>>

Chalked on the wet kerb, in a hand that has written it many times:
//one// — the bones take three of your wits.
//two to five// — they take one.
//six, seven// — they let you be.
//eight// — ten coins, and the bone-man almost smiles.
//nine to eleven// — they give one back.
//twelve// — they give three.

<<if not $bonesAppraised>>
<<linkreplace "Stop, and do the arithmetic before you throw">><<nobr>><<set $bonesAppraised to true>>
<<if setup.statCheck("int", 11)>>
You make yourself stand and count it properly. The taking-faces outnumber the giving-faces by
one — four that cost you against three that pay you back — so over enough throws the gutter
wins your mind a sliver at a time, slow and sure, and the lone coin-face is only bait to keep
you crouched while it does. Ten coins a wit, and the gutter always collects. You could walk now,
clear-eyed — and that clear-eyedness is the most expensive thing you still own down here.
<<else>>
You try to hold the faces in your head and they slide. There's a coin on the eight — you keep
landing on that, the coin — and the rest is just marks in chalk, and the marks say
give-and-take, give-and-take, and it looks, the more you stare, like a game a careful man could
come out of ahead. It looks like that. It is not like that.
<</if>>
<</nobr>><</linkreplace>>
<</if>>

Your wits stand at <<= $scores.int>>.
<<if $scores.int lt setup.WITLESS_INT>>
You are at the bottom of yourself now. The bones cannot take three wits you no longer have — so
the next time they come up asking for three, they will reach past the wits that aren't there and
take the rest of you instead. You can throw, if you like. You can always throw. The gutter is
patient either way.
<<elseif $scores.int lte (setup.WITLESS_INT + 3)>>
There is not much of you left to throw. You can feel how little — a thinness behind the eyes, a
word you reached for just now and didn't find. A few more bad casts and there will be nothing
left here the bones can be paid in but you.
<</if>>

<<link "Crouch down and throw the bones">>
<<set $bonesLast to setup.castBones()>>
<<if $bonesLast.witless>><<goto "EndingWitless">><<else>><<goto "TheGutterBones">><</if>>
<</link>>
<<link "Stand up, keep what wits you have, and go back to the games">><<goto "TheGames">><</link>>


:: EndingWitless [ending]
<<run setup.recordRun("witless")>>
The bones come up on the one — three wits the gutter wants, the way you knew they might and threw
anyway, and you have not three left in you to give it. So it reaches past the wits that aren't
there and takes the rest: the last of the thing that did the worrying goes down the grate with the
runoff, and you feel the gutter take it, the final sliver, the part that was keeping count.

The bone-man looks up. He looks up at last, because there is nothing left in your face to pitch
to, and he chalks one short mark on the kerb to score you — a mark like all the others, a mark
you watch him make and cannot read. You crouch there a while longer, because crouching is what
you are doing. After a while you are not waiting for anything. After a while someone steps over
you to throw, and you do not look up, and the cup tips, and the bones go into the gutter, and
that sound is enough. That sound is plenty.

//You threw your mind into the gutter a sliver at a time, and the gutter kept it, and what's
left of you keeps the bone-man company at the edge of the lights. The carnival has a place for
the witless, too. It is the gutter.//

<<link "Again">><<run setup.restartCarry()>><</link>>


:: TheLostAndFound [carnival]
A lost-and-found booth, piled to its roof with everything the carnival's visitors put
down and didn't pick back up — coats and bags and shoes and spectacles and, in a jar
on the counter, the small valuables, the rings and watches and coin, kept honestly for
owners who, the booth's keeper says with a sympathetic tilt, do so rarely come back to
claim them. You may help yourself, it says. Finders keepers. It's not as if, it does
not say, the losers are using them.

<<if $lostFoundLooted>>
You've taken the loose coin from the jar. The coats and the shoes you leave, and try
not to size them, and do, a little, despite yourself.
<<else>>
In the jar on the counter sits the small unclaimed change, the rings and watches and
coin of owners who do so rarely come back — yours for the taking, the keeper says,
finders keepers, whenever the thought of it brings you back to the booth.
<</if>>

<<if not $keeperTalked>>
The keeper minds the booth with a patient, sympathetic air, and because it has inventoried everyone
who ever passed through the Shambles and set something down, it has — it will tell you, if you ask —
the one story it owns of a thing that came back to claim, and walked back out.
<<linkreplace "Ask the keeper if anyone ever comes back to claim">><<nobr>><<set $keeperTalked to true>>
//Once,// it says, pleased to be asked. //Only the once. Came in carrying //fire// — a branch off the
one tree that burns away down at the bottom of everything and is never the less for it, the tree the
diggers won't go near. He'd broken a piece off and brought it //up,// and it burned in his fist the
whole way and never went out and never burned him.// The keeper tilts its head. //And up top, where the
fat sits across the pipes and seals the way out — he set the fire to it, and the fat //went,// and so
did he. Out and gone. The only one.// A sympathetic little sigh. //Left his coil of rope, mind. They
always leave something.//
<</nobr>><</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
The keeper has told you the one good story it has, and goes back to sorting the coats nobody will
claim — sizing them, gently, against the day you might.
<</if>>

<<link "On, to a vendor's till">><<goto "TheTill">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the wheel">><<goto "TheWheel">><</link>>


:: TheTill [carnival]
A vendor has stepped away from its stall — the cotton-candy spinner, or the thing that
sells the candy that is not cotton — and left the till sitting open, a square of dull
gleam in the carnival glare, the one careless thing in a place that careless-seems on
purpose. The spinning-drum still turns, abandoned, winding sugar into the air over an
empty counter.

Come up to it and the drawer is full: the day's takings just lying there, the cold coin
of a hundred small carnival pleasures bought and half-regretted, stacked loose and uncounted
where any passing hand could close on it. The vendor is nowhere. The next stall's barker has
his back turned. The crowd flows past without a single face angling your way.

Nobody is watching. Nobody, in the Shambles, ever seems to be watching — and standing here
over the open drawer with the easy coin an arm's-length off, you have learned by now that
*nobody watching* is not at all the same thing, down here, as not being seen.

<<if $tillLooted>>
You've lifted the loose coin from the open till. You did not, you notice, feel watched
doing it, and that absence is the most watched you have felt all day.
<<else>>
The drawer sits open and unminded, the day's takings just lying there — the cold coin of
a hundred small carnival pleasures bought and half-regretted, there for the lifting if you
come back while the vendor's still stepped away.
<</if>>

<<link "To a shuttered quiet booth">><<goto "TheQuietBooth">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the lost-and-found">><<goto "TheLostAndFound">><</link>>


/* ---------- REST ---------- */

:: TheQuietBooth [carnival]
At the midway's edge there is a booth that has closed — shutters down, lamp dark, the
barker gone wherever barkers go, a pocket of stillness in the carnival's roar. You
duck into its awning-shadow and the noise drops to a muffled surf, and for the first
time since the teeth you are not being sold to, not being shown a wonder, not being
invited to step inside or spin again or join the show. Just a person in a doorway,
out of the lights, while the fair goes on without them.

In the shade you can finally see the carnival sidelong instead of head-on, and from here
the lit row looks smaller, tireder, the bunting frayed, the colours cheaper — a thing run
by exhausted hands for exhausted reasons, the way every fair looks from behind. The booth
at your back is just boards and dust and an old chalked price-list nobody will read tonight.
Nothing here wants anything from you. It is so quiet you can hear your own breath.

You sit on the booth's step and breathe, and the carnival's pull eases its hand off
you, finger by finger, and you remember that the fun was never free and never yours and
never the point. The rest steadies you. It is the most valuable thing in the Shambles
precisely because it is the one thing nobody here is trying to sell.
<<if $hp lt $maxhp>><<run setup.passTime(setup.REST_TIME)>><</if>><<set $hp to $maxhp>>

<<link "Back out into the midway">><<goto "TheMidway">><</link>>


/* ---------- SNARES (bad ends) ---------- */

:: TheFreakShow [carnival]
The sideshow tent is smaller and closer than the big top, lit a deep warm red that closes
around you like a held breath, and the ringmaster's voice draws you through the flap —
//the wonders, the marvels, the human curiosities, step inside, you've earned it.// The
red light and the low ceiling and the velvet make of the whole tent one long throat, and
you go down it the way you go down everything in the Shambles: because it is easier than
not.

Along the throat, on their little stages, behind their little ropes, are the exhibits — and
walking the row you see what each one is. Every wonder is a person remade into a marvel, a
body edited into an attraction, and every one of them is *content*: each one waves to the
crowd with whatever it has left to wave, each one settled, each one having found — the
placards explain, in a cheerful hand — a kind of peace in being looked at instead of
hunted. They are not in pain. That is the worst thing about them. They look //relieved.//

And there is an empty stage at the end of the row. There is a placard on it, blank, waiting
for a name. There is, you understand too slowly, room for one more wonder — the row was
built with the gap in it, has been holding the gap open this whole time — and you have come
so far, and you are so tired of being a mark and not a marvel.

<<link "Step onto the empty stage. Become a wonder.">><<goto "EndingExhibit">><</link>>
<<link "Get out of the sideshow. Back into the lights.">>
  <<soothe 5>>
  <<goto "TheBigTop">>
<</link>>


:: TheHallOfMirrors [carnival]
A hall of mirrors stands at the end of the attractions, and you go in because everyone
goes in, and the door swings shut on the carnival behind you and there is only glass: walls
of it, corridors of it, a cold silver maze that throws your own dim shape back at you from
every angle at once and gives you no way to tell which dark gap is a turn and which is just
another version of you, waiting.

You work deeper in with your hands out, and the glass does to you what carnival glass does
— makes you tall, makes you small, makes you wide and warped and wrong, a hundred ruined
yous sliding along beside you as you go. It is the oldest trick in any fair and you know it
for one, and still the multiplying does its slow work on the nerves: somewhere in here is
the way out, and somewhere in here are only more of you, and you are losing track of which
reflection is the real you doing the walking.

<<if setup.isDim()>>
And then you find the one that has stopped copying. It holds still when you move; it smiles when
you don't; it beckons, and pours warmth at you through the silver, and mouths something slow and
coaxing — an offer, shaped careful and tempting against the glass. You watch it make the offer
and you do not understand the offer. There is no thread in you for it to pull on; the bargain
needs a mind to land in and finds the house empty. The smile curdles. It mouths the thing again,
slower, as though slowness were the trouble, and you scratch yourself and look at the ceiling,
and at last it gives you up in plain disgust — flicks something small and bright out through the
silver at you, the way you'd toss a coin to be rid of a beggar, and turns its back to wait for a
sharper mark.
<<set _flick to 5 + random(0, 4)>><<set _got to setup.gainCoins(_flick)>>
You gather what it flicked off the cold floor<<if _got gt 0>> — <<= _got>> coins<<else>>, though the coins curdle to rubbish in your fist<</if>>, and blunder on out, no lighter than you
came in — which, in this hall, where everyone leaves a little of themselves grinning in the
glass, is the same as walking out a winner.

<<link "Out the far door, none the wiser, none the lighter">>
  <<goto "ThePuppetShow">>
<</link>>
<<else>>
And then you find the one that has stopped copying. It holds still when you move. It smiles
when you don't. It is, you realize, the version of you the Shambles would prefer — relaxed,
unhurried, *staying* — and it beckons from the glass with enormous warmth, offering to swap,
to let you be the easy reflected one for a while and it the tired one doing the walking.

<<linkreplace "Press your hand to the glass. Trade places, just for a rest.">>
<<corrupt "head" "pig" 24>>
You press your palm to the cold glass and the reflection presses back, and something
passes through the silver the wrong way — and you keep your side of the mirror, you keep
walking, you keep being the tired one, but lighter now by exactly the weight the
reflection took, a portion of you left grinning in the glass for the next mark to meet,
wearing your face, having your rest.
<</linkreplace>>

<<link "Find the way out of the mirrors">>
  <<soothe 4>>
  <<goto "ThePuppetShow">>
<</link>>
<</if>>


/* ---------- COMBAT ---------- */

:: TheMenagerie [carnival]
The menagerie is the carnival's zoo: a long aisle of cages under a sagging canopy, the
captured and the bred and the merely enormous pacing their bars, two-headed things and
boneless things and things that are only a wet shifting in the straw — paced lively for
the crowd by barkers who jab them through the bars with poles when their misery threatens
to bore. The smell is the one thing here the barkers can't paint over: cage-stink,
dung and fear and old straw.

Walk the aisle and the inmates track you, every captive head turning to follow the one
visitor who is not, yet, on the wrong side of a bar — and partway down the row you see why
the others have gone tense. One cage stands open. Its bar is sprung, its straw scattered out
across the aisle, its door swinging on a slow creak, and whatever was in it is no longer in
it.

It is loose among the cages: a rat-man, one of the upright kind from the deep belly, hauled
up here to be an exhibit and plainly unimpressed by the career change. It is between you and
the way you came, and it has stopped its pacing, and it has fixed on you — the nearest soft
thing in the whole menagerie that isn't behind bars.

<<if $menagerieCleared>>
The escaped exhibit lies where you put it down, and a barker is already dragging it back
toward its cage by the heels, grumbling, to be a wonder again whether it wants to or not.
The menagerie pages on, lively, behind its bars.

<<link "On, to the carousel">><<goto "TheCarousel">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the games">><<goto "TheGames">><</link>>
<<else>>
<<scentof "ratman">>
<<link "Put the loose exhibit down before it reaches you">>
  <<run setup.startFight("ratman", "TheMenagerieCleared")>>
  <<goto "Combat">>
<</link>>
<<link "Back to the games, away from the loose cage">><<goto "TheGames">><</link>>
<</if>>


:: TheMenagerieCleared [carnival]
/* lands here after the menagerie fight; sets the flag, then folds onward */
<<set $menagerieCleared to true>>
You put the rat-man down among the cages, and the caged things go briefly, horribly
quiet — a recognition passing along the bars — and then the barkers resume their patter
and the crowd its delight, the brief violence folded back into the show as just another
act, applauded, already forgotten.

<<link "On, to the carousel">><<goto "TheCarousel">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the games">><<goto "TheGames">><</link>>


/* ---------- PASSAGE / ATMOSPHERE FILLER ---------- */

:: TheButchersStall [carnival]
The butcher's stall does the briskest trade on the midway, and you smell it before you
reach it — not rot, but the clean cold copper of a good butcher's, blood and sawdust and
chilled meat — a long counter of cuts under hanging lamps, a queue of cheerful customers,
and behind it the butcher itself: a wide red cheerful thing in a clean apron, working the
crowd in the singsong of butchers everywhere. //Lovely bit of cheek,// it calls, //lovely
bit of heart, who's for a nice piece of somebody.//

Come up to the glass and the cuts are laid out the honest way, the named way, the way a
real butcher names a real animal — cheek and heart and shin and shoulder, labelled in a
neat chalked hand, the marbling good, the colour right. It is only on the second look, the
look you take a beat too long, that the names settle in properly and you understand that
they are the names //you// have, that the animal being named, cut and chalked and wrapped,
is the one you see in mirrors.

The crowd buys happily. The butcher wraps each cut in paper with a wink and a word of
cooking advice, *low and slow, mind, or you'll toughen it.* And it is the most ordinary
stall in the whole Shambles — no glamour, no barker's lie, no rigged wheel, just an honest
tradesman doing honest trade — which is to say it is the worst.

<<if $coins gte 2>>
<<linkreplace "Buy a nice piece of somebody, and eat it (2 coins)">>
<<set $coins to $coins - 2>><<set _h to random(3, 6)>><<set $hp to Math.min($maxhp, $hp + _h)>><<set _amt to random(5, 9)>><<corrupt "torso" "pig" _amt>>
The butcher wraps it warm and you eat it there at the counter, because you are starving and it is —
God help you — //good//, rich and familiar and exactly the thing, and it settles the shaking and
fills the hollow in you (<<= _h>> vigor) and leaves behind, under that, what the carnival feeds
everyone: a little more of the pig, rubbed in from the inside, glad to be eaten home.
<</linkreplace>>
<<else>>//(A nice piece of somebody runs 2 coins. You're short.)//<</if>>

<<link "Past, toward a confectioner">><<goto "TheConfectioner">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the midway">><<goto "TheMidway">><</link>>


:: TheConfectioner [carnival]
A confectioner's cart stands in the alley-mouth, all spun sugar and bright paper twists and
a little brass bell, the cheeriest small thing you have seen since the teeth — and therefore
the one you are most afraid of, because you cannot place a single thing it sells. The sweets
are heaped in colours that don't occur, pulled and folded and dusted, glistening under a
striped awning, and the smell coming off the cart is sweet enough to ache.

Closer, the trade reveals itself: the cart's whole custom is the children that are not
children, who throng it sticky-handed and delighted, coins clutched in fists, faces upturned,
and the confectioner serving them tirelessly, twist after twist, with the bottomless patience
of someone who has all the time there is. None of the grown things buy here. Only the small
ones. Only the ones easiest to please.

//Try a little something,// the confectioner says, catching you looking, and holds out a
twist of something that smells of nothing you have ever smelled and everything you have ever
wanted. //First one's always free.// A pause, a paper-dry little smile. //That's how we get
you.// It laughs, to show it's a joke. It is not entirely a joke.

<<linkreplace "Take the free sample — first one's free">>
<<set _h to random(2, 5)>><<set $hp to Math.min($maxhp, $hp + _h)>><<set _amt to random(4, 8)>><<corrupt "torso" "pig" _amt>>
You take the twist of impossible colour and eat it, and it is the best thing you have ever tasted —
it tastes of being a child and being safe and being home — and it does you good (<<= _h>> vigor) the
way a thing this sweet shouldn't, and under the sweetness, settling in for the long stay, is the
carnival itself, taken willingly, first one free and the rest already paid for.
<</linkreplace>>

<<link "Through to the back alleys">><<goto "TheBackAlleys">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the butcher's stall">><<goto "TheButchersStall">><</link>>


:: TheCarousel [carnival]
A carousel turns slow at the heart of the games, and its mounts are not horses — they
are the menagerie's overstock, the grown and the bred and the broken-to-saddle, fixed to
their poles and going round and round to the calliope's slightly-wrong waltz, and the
children that are not children ride them shrieking with a joy that is, unbearably, real.
It is beautiful, in the carnival's way. The mounts' eyes follow you as they pass, round
and round, patient, and one of them — a long pale thing with too many knees — is, you're
fairly sure, watching for whether you'll get on.

<<linkreplace "Get on. Ride it round, just the once.">>
<<corrupt "legL" "pig" 5>>
You climb onto the long pale mount with too many knees and it carries you round to the wrong waltz,
and it is wonderful, it is genuinely wonderful, the lights wheeling and the not-children shrieking
joy — and when you climb down your legs have learned the going-round of it, the patient saddle-broke
sway, and a little of the mount has climbed up into you, to take your place when you tire of standing.
<</linkreplace>>

<<if not $wranglerKnown>>
A stooped operator works the carousel's brake, catches your eye, and is glad of someone to talk shop
to — proud of the ride, chatty, and entirely willing to explain how a thing becomes a mount.
<<linkreplace "Ask the operator where the mounts come from">><<nobr>><<set $wranglerKnown to true>>
//These? Menagerie overstock, every one. You break 'em to the saddle slow — a turn at a time, round and
round, till the going-round's all they know and they stop fighting the pole.// It pats the long pale
mount as it passes. //Funny thing: the line between a rider and a mount is just //how many turns you
take.// Ride enough and your legs learn the waltz, and one morning you don't get off — you get //fixed
on,// and the next fresh face is riding //you.// No cruelty in it. Just turns.// It winks. //You've a
sway starting already, if you don't mind me saying.//
<</nobr>><</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
The operator gives you a nod and a //back for another turn?// — and notes, not unkindly, the sway you
carry now, the carousel's wrong waltz already settling into the way you stand.
<</if>>

<<link "On, to a fortune-teller's tent">><<goto "TheFortuneTeller">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the menagerie">><<goto "TheMenagerie">><</link>>


:: TheFortuneTeller [carnival]
The fortune-teller's tent is hung with the usual velvet and stars, the cloth heavy enough
to swallow the midway's noise to a hush the moment the flap falls behind you, and the teller
inside is the usual shrouded shape over the usual clouded glass, a beckoning hand and a low
//sit, sit, give me your hand.// Everything about it is the cheap stock-in-trade of every
fairground seer you have ever walked past and not believed.

<<if setup.isDim()>>
<<if $fortuneClue>>
The shrouded shape turns your way and finds you, still, beyond its reach — the one mark it cannot
frighten — and there is nothing more it can do for a mind like yours. It gave you the one true thing
that would fit, and it fit, and that is the whole of its trade with you. //Mind the fat,// it says, the
candle gutters, and the seance is over.
<<else>>
You give it your hand, and it reads your past back to you — the night by the drain, the sack coming
down, the long way down after — surgically, the way it reads everyone. And then it turns your palm to
the candle to tell you the rest, the part that has not happened yet, and the patter drops out of its
voice, and gently, exactly, it begins to tell you how this ends.

And it slides off you like water off oilcloth. You hear the words. You cannot make them into a shape.
A future is a thing you hold in the mind and turn over, and there is less and less of your mind to turn
it in, so the worst news a soul could be handed simply will not go in — it beads on the surface of you
and runs off and is gone. The teller stops. The shroud turns your way, and for the first time the
terrible gentleness is for *itself*: it has met the one mark it cannot frighten, and that frightens
*it*, a little, in whatever it keeps where a heart would go.

Then it does the thing it does for no one with the wit left to be wounded by knowing. It leans close
and gives you, in place of the doom, the one true thing small enough to stick — shaped plain and slow
to fit what is left of you. //The way up is sealed over with fat,// it says, //and fat takes a fire.
Carry one up. That much you can hold.// And you can. It is small enough. It stays.
<<set $fortuneClue to true>>
<</if>>
<<else>>
You give it your hand because that is what the tent is for, expecting the patter — the tall
dark warnings, the soft flattering nothings — and instead it tells you your fortune *right*.
Not vaguely right; not flatteringly right. Specifically, surgically right: about the night
by the drain and the sack coming down and the long way down after, about the bargains and
the marks, about things you have told no one because there has been no one to tell, named
back to you in order.

Then it turns your palm to the candlelight, and goes quiet, and the patter drops out of its
voice entirely. And it tells you the rest — the part that hasn't happened yet — in a voice
gone suddenly, terribly gentle, the way you'd gentle your voice for someone you had bad news
for. You take your hand back fast. Not fast enough. Not nearly fast enough to un-hear how
this is likely to end.

<<linkreplace "Turn your palm back. Ask to hear the rest.">>
<<corrupt "head" "pig" 4>>
You give it your hand again and it tells you the rest, all of it, the exact shape of how this ends,
and the knowing goes into you and cannot be un-known — and the worst of it is the small relief that
comes with it, the relief of a thing decided, a part of you glad to stop fighting a finish it can
finally see.
<</linkreplace>>
<</if>>

<<link "On, to a puppet show">><<goto "ThePuppetShow">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the carousel">><<goto "TheCarousel">><</link>>


:: ThePuppetShow [carnival]
A puppet booth plays to a rapt ring of the not-children, who sit cross-legged and upturned
in the lamplight, still as a held breath: a little stage, a little curtain, a little
painted proscenium, and behind it the clatter and squeak of the puppets working through the
old story — the one every culture has, in some form, the one about the foolish traveller and
the clever devil and the bargain and the price.

Find a place at the back of the ring and watch a while, and the unease creeps up slowly: the
puppets are good. Are very good. Are too good. Their small wooden faces are too expressive by
half, catching the lamplight and turning it into something that looks, for whole beats at a
time, like feeling; their jointed hands are too sure; and there are no strings you can find,
search the dark over the booth as you might, no hands you can place.

And when the devil-puppet turns at the climax to address the audience directly, to deliver
the moral, it does not look at the ring of rapt not-children. It looks, unmistakably and only,
at //you// — finds you at the back without a moment's searching, holds your eye, and winks a
small wooden wink. The not-children laugh and clap, delighted, and do not seem to see that the
show has, for one beat, been entirely about the one real person in the crowd.

<<linkreplace "Stay for the next show. And the next.">>
<<corrupt "head" "pig" 4>>
You stay, because the puppets are so good, and the devil-puppet plays the next show and the next to
you and only you, winking you deeper in, and somewhere in the third or fourth telling you stop being
the foolish traveller watching the story and start being a small wooden thing //in// it, jointed and
sure and grinning, and you have to make your wooden hands let go of the rail to leave.
<</linkreplace>>

<<link "Into a hall of mirrors">><<goto "TheHallOfMirrors">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the fortune-teller">><<goto "TheFortuneTeller">><</link>>


/* ---------- LAYER-6 BAD END ---------- */

:: EndingExhibit [ending]
<<run setup.recordRun("taken")>>
You step up onto the empty stage, because you are so tired of being the mark and the prey
and the thing the carnival is for, and because the wonders on their little stages look so
*restful*, so done with running, and because the ringmaster's voice says //there it is,
there's our new wonder// with a warmth that, after everything, undoes you.

The crowd you could never quite see turns to look, and seeing is the trick, seeing is the
whole apparatus: under the lights, looked at, applauded, you feel the editing begin — gentle,
expert, the body remade by attention into a marvel worth the ticket — and it does not hurt,
and the placard fills in with a name that is almost yours, and you wave to the crowd with
whatever you have left to wave, and find, the placards promised and the placards were right,
a kind of peace in being looked at instead of hunted.

//You stepped into the lights and became a wonder. The Shambles always has room for one more.
You will be looked at forever, and never again hunted, and that is the deal, and you took it.//

<<link "Again">><<run setup.restartCarry()>><</link>>


/* ---------- THE CAGE ACT (willing exploitation: coin for oddity; the barker's 'help' is the trap) ---------- */

:: TheCageAct [carnival]
<<if $pressedReturn>>
They march you up the menagerie row and a barker-overseer points you at an empty cage without a word
of its usual patter — this isn't a sale, it's a shift. //One turn,// it conveys, with a jerk of the
head. //Then you're nobody's problem but your own again.// No contract, no binding; just the work,
and the deep's small cut of you for doing it.

<<linkreplace "Do the turn they marched you here for">>
<<set _ps to setup.doPressedShift()>>
You climb in and give them their oddity — lurch, gibber, the whole sad show — and the part wears a
little more <<= _ps.cls>> into you before the crowd thins and the overseer waves you off. Pressed
work never pays a coin; it only takes its degree of you. Then they turn you loose.

<<link "Back to it">><<set _to to $pressedReturn>><<set $pressedReturn to "">><<goto _to>><</link>>
<</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
A barker — striped and enormous and warm as a bath — has clocked you for exactly what you are and
steers you, all fellowship and easy confidence, toward an empty cage at the back of the menagerie
row. //Here's the thing, friend,// it says. //Crowd loves an oddity, and you've got the look.//

<<if $corruption gte 70>>
It looks you over the way a butcher looks at a good carcass — frank, appreciative, unhurried.
//Half a wonder already, you are,// it says, almost tender. //Wasted out there walking around like a
paying customer. Get in where you belong. Give 'em a turn. Pocket the coin.//
<<elseif $corruption gte 35>>
//More than half-there, that look,// it says, taking you in. //The deep's well into you now — anyone
can see it; you'll barely need the paint. Get in, give 'em a turn, pocket the coin.//
<<else>>
//Half-there already,// it says, //a smudge of the deep still on you, new as you are. Get in, give
'em a turn, pocket the coin.//
<</if>>

//No tricks. Well —// it grins //— one trick. Looking the part. And I can help you with that.//

<<if $workOwed and $workStation == "cage">>
But there is no //talking you into// it tonight, and you both know it. You are signed to this
cage; the binding brought you back here the moment the world put you down, the way it always
will, and the barker is less a tempter now than an overseer with a warm voice. The menagerie
row's gate will not open for you — you can feel the curse holding it, patient and absolute —
until you have given them their turn. A shift is owed. The deep keeps its books.

<<linkreplace "Climb in and work off the shift you owe">>
<<set _s to setup.doShift()>><<set _pay to (setup.wearing("motley") ? random(6, 10) : random(3, 6))>><<set _got to setup.gainCoins(_pay)>>
<<if $corruption gte 70>>
You climb in and do not so much perform as //stand there//, and it is enough, because there is
so little acting left to do — you ARE most of what they came to see. The crowd shudders happily
and throws coin; the barker has stopped coaching you, because there is nothing left to coach.
<<elseif $corruption gte 35>>
You climb in and find you don't have to ham it the way you did the first time. The lurch comes on
its own now; the gibber sits easier in your throat than your own voice has lately. The crowd loves
it, and the line between working the part and being it gets harder to find than it was last shift.
<<else>>
You climb in and give them their oddity — you lurch and gibber and //play// the thing they came to
see — and the crowd loves it and throws coin, and the part wears its groove a little deeper into you.
<</if>>
You came out <<if _got gt 0>><<= _got>> coin<<if _got neq 1>>s<</if>> richer<<else>>with a fistful of coin that curdles to rubbish before you can spend it<</if>>, and the debt, for this return, is
paid; you feel the curse on the gate ease, and the row opens to you again — until the next time the
world knocks you down and the binding reels you back in.
<<if _s and _s.leather>>

And there is this. You have put in your shifts now, and the deep keeps its books both ways: the
barker fetches you a suit of tanned leathers from a hook in the back — //work gear//, it would say,
if it said anything — and waves off your surprise. They are real armour, broken-in and yours.
//(You've worked the cage <<= _s.shifts>> times — free leather armour issued.)//
<</if>>

<<link "Climb out of the cage, back to the stage">><<goto "TheStage">><</link>>
<<link "Out to the big top">><<goto "TheBigTop">><</link>>
<</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
<<if setup.wearing("motley")>>
You are wearing the motley — the patched, parti-coloured fool's suit the barker fits its oddities
in. It is warmer than it was, and harder to find the edge of.
<<elseif setup.has("motley")>>
<<linkreplace "Put the motley back on">>
<<run setup.equip("motley")>>
You pull the patched motley back on, and it settles against you like it never came off — because in
the ways that matter, the barker would say, it didn't.
<</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
<<linkreplace "Let the barker fit you for the part — take the motley">>
<<corrupt "torso" "pig" 14>><<run setup.equip("motley")>>
The barker dresses you with its own huge gentle hands — a motley off a hook, the patched
parti-coloured suit they put on fools and fairground freaks, pieced from a hundred acts that wore it
before you and still warm from the last — and it does not so much go //on// as go //in//, the cloth
settling against you and then under you, and when the barker turns the cage's little mirror to you,
you look — genuinely, convincingly — //odd//. //There he is,// it beams. //Now you'll pull a proper crowd.//
<</linkreplace>>
<</if>>

A turn in the cage is always there for the taking. How much of a show you make of it — and how much
of the part you let take root — is your own to decide; the crowd pays for what it's given, and it
gives most for the costume.

<<linkreplace "Give them a quick caper — a lurch, a gibber, enough to shake a coin loose">>
<<set _seg to setup.SEGMENTS.random()>><<set _amt to random(2, 4)>><<corrupt _seg "pig" _amt>>
<<set _pay to random(3, 5)>><<set _got to setup.gainCoins(_pay)>>
You give them the cheap version — a lurch, a gibber, the bare minimum that reads as oddity — and a
thin crowd throws a thin scatter of coin, <<if _got gt 0>><<= _got>> of it,<<else>>which rots to rubbish the moment you scoop it up,<</if>> and only a little of the part sets its
hook. Barely working; barely worth it. //Barely// is the trap the place is built on.
<</linkreplace>>

<<if setup.wearing("motley")>>
<<linkreplace "Work the crowd in the motley — really sell the oddity, pull a proper house">>
<<set _seg to setup.SEGMENTS.random()>><<set _amt to random(6, 10)>><<corrupt _seg "pig" _amt>>
<<set _pay to random(9, 14)>><<set _got to setup.gainCoins(_pay)>>
<<if $corruption gte 60>>
In the motley you barely perform at all — you simply //are// it, and the cage is only a frame around a
thing the crowd already half-recognises. They press to the bars and shudder and rain coin<<if _got gt 0>>, <<= _got>>
of it,<<else>> — but it curdles to rubbish in your hands,<</if>> and the barker has stopped coaching you between turns, because there is nothing left in you to
coach. The costume is warmer every time, and harder to find the edge of, and one of these turns there
will be no edge left to find.
<<else>>
You work it properly this time, the way the costume lets you — the full lurching gibbering show, the
crowd pressed to the bars and loving it, coin coming down like weather. <<if _got gt 0>>You pocket <<= _got>>, the
easiest money in hell,<<else>>You pocket what comes down, but it curdles to rubbish before you can spend it,<</if>> and the part wears its groove that much deeper for the selling of it; the
motley goes a little further in, glad, as ever, of the room.
<</if>>
<</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
Without the motley the crowd only ever gets the cheap version — a proper house wants the costume on
you, and the costume, you are beginning to grasp, wants rather more of you than the cloth.
<</if>>

<<link "Climb out of the cage, back to the stage">><<goto "TheStage">><</link>>
<<link "Out to the big top">><<goto "TheBigTop">><</link>>
<</if>>
<</if>>
